---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { site } from '../lib/site';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description="Product and program leader working across health technology, regulated fintech and hyperscale cloud security."
>
  <section class="wrap wrap--prose about">
    <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
    <h1>I build products for technical teams working inside regulated, high trust environments.</h1>

    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        My work has moved across health care, elections, fintech and hyperscale cloud. The
        setting has changed, but the pattern has stayed the same. People are asked to do
        important work inside systems that are complex, fragmented and risky. They need tools
        that make the right action clear. They need evidence they can trust. They need
        workflows that reduce noise instead of adding more of it.
      </p>

      <p>
        I started in Ghana as a founder. At ClaimSync we built an electronic medical record
        and claims automation platform for health care providers and insurers. That taught me
        that product work is not really about screens or features. It is about understanding
        how people make decisions when the stakes are high. A good product changes the
        behaviour of a system. In our case, better digital claims workflows helped reduce
        fraud by about 20% and created enough value for the company to be acquired.
      </p>

      <p>
        At mPharma I built Bloom, the pharmacy operating system. The problem was
        basic and serious. Pharmacies could not reliably keep the right medicines in stock,
        and patients paid for it. We built inventory and operating tools for more than 60
        pharmacies across five or more countries and cut stock-outs by nearly half. mPharma
        relaunched Bloom in 2020 and by the following year it was running in over 250
        pharmacies and had been deployed to run a national medicines supply chain. That work
        shaped how I think about product leadership. The best products are not clever for
        their own sake. They remove a constraint that people feel every day.
      </p>

      <p>
        At Wise I worked on regional expansion across the Middle East and Africa. I learned
        how much judgment is required when regulation, operations, banking partners and
        customer needs all meet at once. Launching Send Money in the UAE through an ADGM
        Money Services licence was not only a market launch. It was a lesson in earning trust
        before scale.
      </p>

      <p>
        At Microsoft my focus has been developer experience, platform compliance and security
        automation. I work on products and operating mechanisms that help engineering teams
        understand their services, improve reliability, produce audit evidence and remediate
        vulnerabilities. The strongest results came when we treated compliance as a product
        problem rather than a reporting problem. We reduced recurring reporting effort by
        about 70%, automated about 80% of incident creation and about 90% of daily SLA
        reporting, and delivered zero PCI and FedRAMP vulnerability audit findings in H1 2025.
      </p>

      <h2>What I optimise for</h2>

      <p>
        Clarity, trust and leverage. Clarity means a team knows what matters and why. Trust
        means the data, the controls and the decisions are auditable. Leverage means removing
        repeated effort so people can spend their time on judgment, design and engineering
        instead.
      </p>

      <h2>How I lead</h2>

      <p>
        I write things down. I listen carefully. I use data without hiding behind it. I build
        operating cadences that survive beyond any one person, including me. I like teams
        where product managers stay close to the work, close to engineers, and honest about
        tradeoffs.
      </p>

      <p>
        I have also stayed hands-on in every role. I have found that you cannot tell whether a
        mechanism is genuinely self-sustaining unless you have built part of the thing that
        sustains it.
      </p>

      <h2>How I write, and who taught me</h2>

      <p>
        Three influences, and I will name them plainly because the debt is obvious to
        anyone who reads much of this site.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>Paul Graham</strong> for the form. Plain prose, one idea worked all the way
        through, no illustration where a sentence will do, and the willingness to publish a
        thought that is not yet fashionable. His essays are the reason this site has no cover
        images and no reading-time badges. If the writing needs decoration, the writing is not
        finished.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>Addy Osmani</strong> for the standard of proof. He writes as a practitioner
        about things he has actually shipped, with the specifics intact, and he does it on a
        cadence. That combination is rarer than it sounds. Most technical writing is either
        specific and abandoned, or regular and empty. The case studies here try to hold his
        line: say what you did, say what it cost, say the number.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>ThoughtWorks</strong> for the discipline of taking a position. The Technology
        Radar is the best artefact in our industry because it does the hard thing: it puts
        practices into rings, signs its name, and dates the judgement so you can hold it to
        account later. Most of us write to stay defensible. The Radar writes to be useful. My{' '}
        <a href="/radar">radar</a> is a straight borrow of that idea, pointed at product
        practice.
      </p>

      <p>
        What the three have in common is that none of them hedge. That is the thing I am
        actually trying to copy.
      </p>

      <h2>What I want next</h2>

      <p>
        The same kind of problem I have kept choosing. Complex systems. Real risk. Many teams.
        A need to turn ambiguity into trusted products that make people better at their work.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="about__foot">
      <h2 class="about__foot-title">Elsewhere</h2>
      <ul class="about__links">
        <li><a href={`mailto:${site.email}`}>{site.email}</a></li>
        <li><a href={site.linkedin} rel="me noopener">LinkedIn</a></li>
        <li><a href="/cv">Curriculum vitae</a></li>
        <li><a href="/speaking">Speaking and mentorship</a></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .about {
    padding-block: var(--space-2xl) var(--space-3xl);
  }

  .about h1 {
    font-size: var(--step-title-lg);
    max-width: 20ch;
    margin-bottom: var(--space-xl);
  }

  .about__foot {
    margin-top: var(--space-2xl);
    padding-top: var(--space-l);
    border-top: var(--rule-weight) solid var(--rule);
  }

  .about__foot-title {
    font-size: var(--step-micro);
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-eyebrow);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--ink-muted);
    font-weight: var(--weight-semibold);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-s);
  }

  .about__links {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: var(--space-m);
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin: 0;
    font-size: var(--step-caption);
  }

  .about__links a {
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-decoration-color: var(--accent-underline);
  }
</style>
